artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a strategic forest planning problem over a long time horizon (typically several decades to a century) on a landscape partitioned into management units. A decision maker must choose harvesting, silvicultural, road-building, and conservation actions in each period to optimize an economic objective (e.g., net present value of timber revenues) subject to operational constraints (sustained yield, flow constraints, capacity, budget).

In addition to the economic objective, the plan must respect environmental and sustainability concerns, including (but not limited to):
(i) biodiversity (e.g., habitat amount, connectivity, species-specific suitability indices);
(ii) watershed condition (e.g., bounds on sediment yield, peak flow, riparian buffers);
(iii) wildlife preservation (e.g., minimum patch sizes, adjacency/green-up constraints, corridor requirements);
(iv) old-growth protection (e.g., minimum area of forest above a given age class, spatial distribution of old-growth);
(v) broader sustainability goals such as carbon storage, ecosystem services, recreation, and social/cultural values.

Many of these concerns are imprecisely defined, may involve conflicting stakeholder preferences, and often require nonlinear, spatial, or stochastic representations.

Problem. Develop a mathematical modeling framework and accompanying solution methodology that:
(a) gives rigorous quantitative representations of the above environmental and sustainability concerns, possibly via fuzzy, probabilistic, multi-criteria, or spatially explicit formulations, suitable for inclusion in a strategic forest planning model;
(b) integrates these representations with classical economic and operational constraints into a single tractable optimization formulation; and
(c) admits solution algorithms capable of producing provably good (optimal or with quantified optimality gap) solutions on instances of realistic size (landscapes with tens of thousands of management units and multi-period horizons).

Specify the modeling assumptions, prove correctness of the formulation, and provide a solution method with complexity or convergence guarantees, together with computational evidence of its scalability.

